// Doc 18 §2.1's `$` and the multi-line pipeline — plan 51.
//
// Both of these work today and neither had an example, which is the whole
// reason plan 51 exists. `$` shipped in doc 18 §11.1's commit `0abcdf0` and
// `examples/lexical.sdx` nevertheless explained its absence by calling it
// unlexed. A construct that is specified, implemented and unexampled is an
// undiscoverable one, and `construct_coverage` now fails when one appears.
//
// Checked by compiling and **running** with zero rustc output, because the
// precedence cases are the ones a golden alone cannot settle: `$` applied to
// the wrong operand still compiles and prints a different number.

fn show(n: i32) -> String {
    format!("{}", n)
}

fn twice(s: String) -> String {
    format!("{}{}", s, s)
}

// `$` is **lower** than every operator, so the sum is the argument and not
// just the `1`. Written with parentheses this is `print (1 + 2)`; the point
// of `$` is that it does not need them.
fn sum_of(a: i32, b: i32) -> String {
    format!("{} + {}", a, b)
}

// `$` is **right**-associative, so this is `twice (show 7)` and never
// `(twice show) 7` — which would not compile, and so is the one case here
// that a wrong lowering cannot hide.
fn nested() -> String {
    twice(show(7))
}

// And it does not swallow a method chain: the receiver of `.len ()` is
// `show 3`, not the whole application.
fn chained() -> usize {
    show(3).len()
}

// The multi-line pipeline. The receiver sits on the `=` line and every
// continuation indents beneath it — which is the one layout that parses, and
// nothing in doc 18 §4 says so. The alternative, with the receiver on its own
// line after `=`, is `RDX0102`.
//
// Four stages, because the argument for `|>` is that a chain reads top to
// bottom in the order the data moves. The emitted Rust is **one line**, which
// is the lowering working — and is also where this example found a defect
// larger than itself.
//
// `generated_rust_is_rustfmt_clean` failed on the first draft of this
// function. The emitter joins a chain onto one line whatever its width, and
// rustfmt breaks a chain wider than `chain_width` (60 by default) across
// lines. No golden had ever caught it: the longest chain in the corpus before
// this file was `a.len() + b.len()`, 21 characters. A fifth stage here takes
// the chain to 73 and the gate goes red.
//
// So this function stays inside 60 deliberately, and the defect is recorded
// rather than worked around in silence — plan 58 makes long chains the normal
// case and is where the emitter learns to break them.
fn evens_doubled(xs: Vec<i32>) -> usize {
    xs.iter().filter(|n| *n % 2 == 0).map(|n| n * 2).count()
}

fn main() {
    println!("{}", 1 + 2);
    println!("{}", sum_of(2, 3));
    println!("{}", nested());
    println!("{}", chained());
    println!("{}", evens_doubled([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6].to_vec()));
}
